/*!
    \page tutorial-project-setup.html
    \title Tutorial 3: The Felgo Project
    \previouspage {Tutorial 2: Install Felgo and Run the Game}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 4: Scenes and the Game Window}

    A Felgo game is a Qt app with a few extra pieces. Here is what they are.

    \section1 Folders

    \table
        \header \li Folder \li Content
        \row \li \c backend \li C++ game core and the classes that expose it to QML
        \row \li \c qml \li Felgo scenes and QML components
        \row \li \c data \li Game rules in JSON
        \row \li \c assets \li Sprites and images
    \endtable

    \section1 CMake

    The core is a plain library. The app target adds QML, data and shaders:

    \quotefromfile CMakeLists.txt
    \skipto qt_add_executable(appCreatureWars
    \printuntil felgo_configure_executable(appCreatureWars)

    \list
        \li \c qt_add_resources puts JSON and images into \c qrc:/, so they work on every
            platform. Example: \c {"qrc:/data/economy.json"}.
        \li \c qt_add_shaders compiles shaders to \c .qsb, which Qt 6 needs.
        \li \c felgo_configure_executable links Felgo and prepares Android and iOS packaging.
    \endlist

    \section1 main.cpp

    Create \c FelgoApplication next to the QML engine and initialize it:

    \quotefromfile main.cpp
    \skipto QApplication app(argc, argv);
    \printuntil felgo.initialize(&engine);

    Pass your C++ object to QML and load the main file:

    \quotefromfile main.cpp
    \skipto engine.setInitialProperties({
    \printuntil });

    \quotefromfile main.cpp
    \skipto felgo.setMainQmlFileName
    \printuntil engine.load

    In QML the object is then a required property:

    \code
    GameWindow {
        required property var gameBackend
    }
    \endcode

    \b {Why not a context property?} \c setContextProperty is the classic way, but required
    properties are checked by the QML compiler and are the recommended Qt 6 style.

    \note The Felgo splash screen and a license message appear until you set
    \c PRODUCT_LICENSE_KEY in \c CMakeLists.txt.
*/
